AWS Snapshot vs AMI – What's the Difference?


Imagine you're using a perfectly configured laptop:
Right apps, perfect wallpaper, all settings tuned.
Now you want the exact same setup on another laptop.
That’s where Snapshot and AMI come in — but they’re not the same.


Snapshot – Backup of storage only
Snapshot is a backup of your EBS volume (storage).
It only stores data, not OS or configs.
You can’t launch EC2 directly from a snapshot.
Think of it like: Maine hard drive ka photo le liya.


AMI (Amazon Machine Image) – Full system template
AMI contains:
  OS
  Snapshot (storage)
  Launch permissions
  EC2 configuration

You can directly launch EC2 instances from it.
Use AMI when you want to create multiple identical servers.
Socho: Poora laptop ka duplicate ban gaya, boot hone ke liye ready — that’s AMI.


Does AMI include EBS?
Yes, AMI includes EBS snapshots.
When you create an AMI from an EC2 instance, AWS automatically takes snapshots of the EBS volumes attached to that instance — including the root volume (OS disk) and any additional volumes.
So jab aap AMI se naya EC2 launch karte ho, wo same EBS volumes ke sath launch hota hai — just like the original setup.



Interview Tip:
Can I create an AMI from a snapshot?
Yes, but you’ll need to define configs and attach it properly.
Visualise. Don’t just memorise.
 Snapshot is just storage.
 AMI = OS + Storage + Config — a full system.
